/*****************************************************************************
                designDocs -- DESIGN DOCUMENTS AS KANT DATA.

    Pure data, no actions (the decoder/jigcorpus/unitTests pattern). NO
    Start()/stop() wrapper -- this is a registry file, not an execution file.
    Registered in incant/setup's fILEs registry as `designDocs`, because
    include() searches no path and an unregistered include fails at exit 0.

    CANONICAL. Each entry in this registry is the live ruling on its subject.
    The markdown file an entry was converted from becomes historical on the day
    the entry lands, and says so in its own header -- exactly one live ruling
    per subject, no ambiguous populations.

    FIRST ENTRY: DisplayDesignHTML. Drafted by Tony offline over 2026-08-10 and
    2026-08-11, reviewed by Tony and Clay, ruled canonical 2026-08-11, and homed
    here that day. Its source text was carried in IncantForms/WorkingOn/incant++
    and is transcribed below with ONE repair, named here rather than left
    silent.

    ⚠ THE REPAIR: THREE MISSING TERMINATING SEMICOLONS, WHICH CHANGED THE TREE
    AND COST NOTHING AT EXIT-STATUS LEVEL. As written, EmissionPrinciple,
    DisplayLayout and DisplayEvents ended with no `;`, so each NESTED what
    followed it instead of ending itself. Measured 2026-08-11 on the as-written
    text -- exit 0, sentinel printed, stderr empty, and this shape:

        DisplayDesignHTML   length 6
            DisplayProvenance · DisplayPipeline · DisplayOutput
            StreamModel       length 3   <- correct
            EmissionPrinciple length 5   <- swallowed DisplayLayout,
                                            DisplayTargets and its three
            DisplayEvents     length 1   <- swallowed DisplayScope

    With the three semicolons added it measures NINE top-level members, with
    StreamModel's three and DisplayTargets' three nested under their own
    parents. That is what docs/displayDesign.md §1-§9 describes, so the intent
    was not in question; only the punctuation was. PARSE-GREEN IS NOT
    SHAPE-CORRECT, and a registry nobody has a verb over yet is exactly where
    that goes unnoticed -- the first reader of this entry would have been a
    consolidation minion, reading a tree that says EmissionPrinciple governs
    layout, targets and events.

    ITS PROVENANCE LIVES IN THE ENTRY, not in this header, so a reader who
    arrives by query rather than by reading the file still gets it. See the
    DisplayProvenance attribute. The superseded document is docs/displayDesign.md
    (recorded SEQ 29). The divergences from that document are rulings and are
    named there: the HTML event fence was widened, the measurer was made a
    protocol parameter, the CSS-reconsider hedge was recorded, and the
    save/restore rejected alternative was recorded inline.

    NO BEHAVIOR IS EXPECTED OF THIS FILE YET. It parses and it loads. A verb
    over it -- the decoder's decodE, or a displayForm dump -- is later work and
    is nobody's task as of this writing.
*****************************************************************************/
register(DesignDocs);
define
    DisplayDesignHTML="Display can have multiple targets. Here we record design decisions on using HTML as a display target. The overriding assumption is: emit an HTML style to handle field layout; no CSS involved, altho that may change based on lessons learned from implementation.":
        DisplayProvenance="Canonical as of 2026-08-11 review (Tony + Clay). Supersedes docs/displayDesign.md (SEQ 29), retained as historical. Changes from the md version are rulings, not drift: HTML event fence widened, measurer made a protocol parameter, CSS-reconsider hedge recorded.";
        DisplayPipeline="Display text comes from an input source, which is a field w/text probably contained in a field buffer. A form is a field that defines how to layout its components for display. The form attributes format field text, setting characteristics such as bgColor, font, ... Output goes where the Display targets; into an HTML file in this case";
        DisplayOutput="Current thinking is display can go into a buffer; into a Display, which is a class akin to a buffer wrapped around a bitmap with methods to write on the bitmap the way Buffer methods write into a buffer. The Display class is intended to feed a window or a page to be printed (the latter is linked into the notion of using print as a typesetting tool).";
        StreamModel="Output is a forward only stream, not a scope tree, with three tiers defined in members below. Rejected alternative, recorded: save/restore style bracketing — same failure class as the door-one bracket, measured SEQ 27 (CLAIM KANT-8, commit 168453d). If forward-only proves insufficient, revisit from here, not from scratch.":
            NamedStyles="The only thing that changes stream state. A named-style set replaces the current style wholesale (absolute, not delta). A style sheet is a GroupItem field of named bundles, defined once and referenced by name. Every named set is a sync point: the current style at any point is the last named set, with no replay. Styles are stored in a Styles register.";
            CurrentStyle="Stream state owned by Display. A style setting is permanent until the next set. No restore exists — no bracket, no seam. Forward only.";
            FieldStyleSetting="Field attributes can merge over the current style for rendering only; ephemeral, never written back. An attribute that bolds a field being displayed does not bold the stream. How this works to be determined.";
        EmissionPrinciple="Display targets receive resolved output; style machinery never crosses the emit boundary. That means output resolution happens in kant (attributes make it happen) before display gets thrown out the emit door.";
        DisplayLayout="Kant owns layout; display targets are paint surfaces differing only in the methods applied to do the painting. Kant needs access to glyph widths for line-breaking, pagination. No target is ever asked to lay anything out. Measurement crosses a protocol boundary: kant asks a measurer for metrics; the measurer is a parameter, as the target is. First measurer is Apple Core Text (small spike, ruled 2026-08-11); the protocol keeps non-Apple consumers open.";
        DisplayTargets="Three currently envisioned: HTML, Window, Print/typesetting":
            HTMLTarget="First Light target. For now constrained to wiki-like static documents. Emitted, resolved, done when written; no JavaScript planned. Simple events (resize, mouse clicks) pass back to kant, which re-lays-out and re-emits as needed — the target reports, kant decides. Exercises source / attributes / target without geometry pressure. Printing to HTML is Display's first customer.";
            WindowTarget="The Full Monty interactive surface. Live events, resize → reflow → repaint, drag-and-drop and whatever else: that machinery belongs here, sorted as we go. Beachhead exists(`Layout`/`Stylish`, `displayFill`). Plenty design work required before implementation.";
            PrintTarget="Full measurement required, pagination. Events not applicable. Design as we go with a try and buy approach because we have no clear target to measure against as yet.";
        DisplayEvents="Mostly an issue for the WindowTarget, however, the HTML target should handle simple events like resize or mouse clicks; scroll events to be considered.";
        DisplayScope="Display is output only, as Buffer is accumulate only. Input handling and layout is form logic.";
    JitFieldMethod="The one named site that owns set-then-call dispatch for a field's compiled method. It is parseMethod's shape transplanted from rules to fields, deliberately, so the tree carries one pattern and not two.":
        JitFieldMethodProvenance="Clay SEQ 27 v2, 2026-08-04. Migrated 2026-08-15 from a 66 line comment block, as the comment-migration pilot. Code site: jitEmitters.rtn, jitFieldMethod.";
        JitFieldMethodContract="Three cases, in the order the code reads them.":
            SlotSet="Slot set means call through the pointer. That is the whole dispatch: no name lookup, no reconstruction from the JiT record, no consultation of CodE or BlocK.";
            SlotEmpty="Slot empty means compile via jitRunAction, then stash both: the function pointer into rStuff.jitMethod, and the emitted IR text into a JiT attribute beside CodE and BlocK.";
            CompileRefused="A refused compile leaves the slot empty and says so. A refusal must not install a pointer and must not be silent; a field that quietly stopped being jitted would read as a field that was never jittable.";
        JitFieldMethodInvariant="SEQ 38 holds by construction here, and this is the place it would have broken. locate is prohibited, not merely unused: action execution resolves no names. The field arrives as the first parameter and everything else is a pointer walk from it, rStuff off the node and the JiT attribute off its own list. If a later change here seems to need locate, that change is misdesigned rather than blocked.";
        JitFieldMethodMechanism="The slot is read and written on definingRule, not on the node that arrives, and that is the whole mechanism rather than a detail. Every call site hands a fresh GroupItem wrapper over one shared GroupBody, which is incant field semantics and not a bug: a field is pointer-shaped storage with value-content semantics, so a reference is its own node. Storing the pointer on the arriving node stores it on a temporary discarded when the statement ends. The store stuck and readback confirmed it; it was written where nothing would look again. definingRule is a pointer walk to the node that owns the children, and parse uses it for exactly this problem in exactly this way. So the half that makes the shape work is the resolution, not the slot: transplanting the slot without the walk produces code that looks right and compiles forever.";
        JitFieldMethodEvidence="Measured 2026-08-04, because the first cut recompiled on every fire. Three calls, field pointer then body pointer: call 1 field 0x1031ccf80 body 0x1031c6380; call 2 field 0x1031df5c0 body 0x1031c6380; call 3 field 0x1031ed9c0 body 0x1031c6380; and definer 0x100eaa3c0 on all three.";
        JitFieldMethodSlotHome="Why the slot's home is materialised here and not at define time. aCTionDefinE zeroes rStuff for a non-rule, so an ordinary attribute reaches this point with no shape struct at all, while the design as ruled says the slot rides the attribute's stuff. Rather than change what define does to every field in the language, the slot's owner mints its own home on the one path that needs it, using the same two lines aCTionDefinE already uses for member terms. Cost is one struct per jitted field, paid on first fire, and the define-time invariant is untouched.";
        JitFieldMethodRejected="Rejected alternative, recorded: reconstructing a callable from the JiT attribute. The JiT attribute is a record and not a cache, written after a successful compile and read by nobody in this function. Reconstructing from it would mean re-entering LLVM to parse text we already hold a pointer to, which is slower, and it would give one fact two homes, the one-channel-one-meaning failure this project has paid for twice.";
        JitFieldMethodReturns="Returns trueResult when the method ran on either path, and null when the compile was refused.";
    KantParser="Incant has a generalized parser accessible from any field so parsing incant is pick a field, run its parse. The general parser works, but is less than efficient. On vacation, in a bar in Amsterdam, a back of napkin design prompted an interesting challenge: use kant (nickname for incant) to write a tailored parse per rule based on the incant grammar. The tailoring is kant code in a write parser field that can be run on demand to deal with grammar changes, essentially generating the equivalent of hand written parse code for every incant rule.":
        KantParserWhy="Incant is designed to be extendable. Its grammar can change accordingly. And as design on a napkin was happening in a bar, incant was developing a Just In Time compiler. The parser redesign and jitting came together to form an objective that evolved into a campaign: use kant to rewrite its parser; verify the new parse code works; jit the new parse code; replace the interpreting parser with the jitted code; and do it with no code file produced as a by product; leaving no trace anything happened to incant aside from parsing got simpler and faster. Bottom line: kant has a parser that can silently rewrite itself to be more efficient.";
        KantParserHow="Incant rules are just incant fields; every component of a rule is an incant field; and kant code is all incant fields (a kant statement is a rule and its components, operators operands et al, are all fields. Thus you can walk a rule and its components using kant statements (the bar napkin design session also produced a design for a kant iterator to help with that). To simplify the walk and parser code generation, we opted to constrain the structure of incant rules to contain only data, only attributes, or only members (in a rule, members are rule options). Incant grammar rules now fit that constraint; it does not constrain how rules work, only how they are written.";
        KantParserFlow="Generating a rule specific parse is a rule walk that generates parse action code -> code gets stashed in the rule -> generalized parser interprets that code and stores it in the rule as a parsed block of code that the interpreter can run to verify the generated parse code works -> once verified the generated parse code gets jitted -> the jitted code then replaces the generalized parser -> field() runs a field method; and for a rule field, that method invokes jitted parse code that did not exist at flow start.";
        InterpretOrCompile="Interpreting and compiling follow parallel paths. Both walk field action code. The code looks the same to interpreter and compiler. Both produce a block of code that can be interpreted. However, the compiler/jitting process also produces a jitted field method to run the field's parse.";
    arrondirNote date="2026-08-20" site="Commands.rtn arrondir" purpose="rounds a value" invoke="as incant command" why="avoid libc symbol collision" beartraps="7 12" description="arrondir is French for round and it works as an incant command that does what round does. round is in libc math.h so trying to use it directly as an incant command would generate a duplicate symbol error on linking.
        Borrowing a word from another language gets around that issue. arrondir is free at C level, causing no linking duplication.
        arrondir is not used in underlying kant methods like opDiv(). Likewise GroupItem getCount() does not use arrondir. opDiv and getCount are C++ methods so they do not need it. Kant has no such option; arrondir allows kant to run round().";
    Incantations="Design entries that belong to a particular INCANTATION rather than to a subsystem, one child per incantation. Minted 2026-08-26 on Tony's suggestion: designDocs was growing a flat list in which a file's operating notes sat at the same level as a subsystem's architecture, and the two answer different questions. A subsystem entry says what the design IS; an incantation entry says how one runnable file WORKS and what it has already been measured to do. The child key is the incantation, never the campaign it serves -- KantParser stays a subsystem entry because the campaign outlives any one file, while the parser form's gates and provenance live here.":
        ParserIncantation="IncantForms/WorkingOn/parser. Walks a rule and everything below it, generates a parse body for each, compiles it, and closes by parsing a specimen through the rule so the run reports an answer rather than a count. parser(Start) is the intended POP and does not pass yet; see ProblemRecords parseSelfRecursion. Reduced from 344 lines to its finished shape 2026-08-26 across two reviewed iterations, Tony ruling by review at each pass.":
            ParserGates="Three phases, two gates, ruled 2026-08-26. Generation always runs. Activation binds the generated body as the rule parse method via setParse and is gated by the activating field. Compilation is gated by the compiling field. A measuring run turns one or both off; a verifying run leaves both on, because a parse method that is never bound is never exercised. Both gates are declared fields in the define block, because a driver writes them and the walk reads them and an undeclared name shared by two actions becomes a per-action local (bear trap 39). Both are tested against a value and never bare, because a bare test on a declared field asks whether it exists and is always true (bear trap 26).";
            ParserGateMeasurement="THE ACTIVATION GATE IS A MEASUREMENT-QUALITY GATE AND CANNOT BE DELETED. Measured 2026-08-26 as two full runs of the walk over Start, one per arm, both completing at exit 0 with the sentinel reached. Activation OFF: 37 refusals, 60 leaf installs, and every refusal names the position it stopped at. Activation ON: 42 refusals, 68 leaf installs, and 36 of the 42 refusals collapse to reached end of input with no position at all. So activation changes both the population the walk reaches and the legibility of every refusal it reports. It is kept as a gate of the compiling family rather than made unconditional. The reason activation is nonetheless ON by default is that verification needs it: with activation off the verifier returns no answer, which is the negative control recorded under KantParserVerification.";
            ParserVerification="The run closes with an answer, not a count. Verification rather than measurement was Tony's word for the target state, 2026-08-26. The driver walks its argument, then parses a specimen string through that same rule and prints what came back, so a run that generated a body which cannot parse is visibly different from one that generated a body which can. The specimen lives in the ruleText field in the define block and the rule arrives as the driver argument, so pointing the file at a different rule is two edits and no restructuring. Little steps for little feet: run it on whichever rule wants verifying, and parser(Start) is the final POP.";
            ParserHangHistory="RETIRED PROVENANCE, migrated out of the parser form 2026-08-26 when the defect it documented stopped reproducing. From 2026-08-19 to 2026-08-25 a walk that called setParse and then compiled the same rule HUNG, capped at 137, and a walk that suppressed setParse completed. The toggle now named activating was minted as the control for exactly that. The discriminator was measured to be setParse itself and not the number of phases: walkPhase does not call setParse and swept 56 clean refusals, and adding setParse to its generator hung it on its first item with zero refusals reported. That retracted the earlier reading, recorded as F-23, which had credited a one-phase versus two-phase split. It is the standing pattern in this project that structural claims hold and causal ones fail. The hang was predicted in writing before it was measured, by F-17a and F-18, both of which named infinite recursion through the parseMethod fork. As of 2026-08-26 the hang does not reproduce: a full walk over Start completes at exit 0 with the sentinel reached, and the closing of the label seam on 2026-08-25 is the change it followed. The five-configuration hang matrix, the two Xcode breakpoint anchors on the arming site and the parseRule entry, and the five inert station fields that carried them were retired with it.";
            ParserExhibitHistory="A NEGATIVE EXHIBIT, RETIRED 2026-08-26, recorded because it settled a question by failing. A pair of drivers compiled the same body text from two provenances, one defined by hand through the define door and one installed by the walk onto the live grammar rule, to test whether provenance was what decided a refusal. BOTH COMPILED. Provenance is not the discriminator, so the confound the pair carried was never worth resolving, and the search moved to setParse, which was the answer. The pair is retired rather than kept because the question it answered is closed and the drivers were the largest measurement surface in the file.";
            ParserDriverShape="THE DRIVER IS AN ACTION AND ITS STOP IS INSIDE IT, and that is not style, it is what makes the file finish. Measured 2026-08-19. Once the walk installs generated parse methods the loader can no longer read the source that FOLLOWS, so a top level walk call followed by a top level stop never reaches its stop: the parser goes back for the next statement, cannot read one, and dies. An action body is parsed once at define time into a cached block, so a driver action is already-parsed code and needs no loader, and calling stop from inside it ends the process before the loader is asked for anything more. The corollary is that the file carries ONE driver line and one only. The old shape exit status was also an accident of what followed it: with dead walk lines sitting below the stop the run exited 0, because the poisoned loader failed to parse them and abandoned the file, which is the documented exit-zero-on-parse-failure path; truncate those same lines and the identical run exits 139. Neither run reached stop and neither said so. That is why the sentinels are on cerr and not on print: stdout is block buffered and a crash loses it, so a stdout sentinel cannot report the crash it exists to detect.";
            ParserRetiredPrediction="A PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTION THAT CAN NO LONGER BE READ, kept as a reasoning trail and not as an open question. Written 2026-08-18, before the run, so it could not be fitted afterwards: after the mint fix gave every rule its own code node, the prune-noPrint census was predicted to move from 17 to 34, derived as 54 generated minus 20 empty-condition bodies. The two census lines it scored against were removed from the walk in a later offline edit, so the instrument is gone and no run can settle it either way. It must not be repaired by re-adding counters to make the number appear. If the question is live again it wants a fresh pre-registration against an instrument that exists.";
    ProblemRecords="The operational history of every problem this project has fought, one entry per PROBLEM. Entries key on the problem, never on the station or the rung that met it, because incant/frontier iterates and its station 4 next month is a different animal from its station 4 today. Where a problem was met is an attribute (station, vintage), not identity. Minted 2026-08-23, Clay brief ratified by Tony the same day, superseding the FrontierStations and Shorthand drafts of that morning, which keyed on the station and carried two schemas.":
        ProblemRecordSchema gloss="the schema itself" status="doctrine" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" description="THE SCHEMA, defined once. SKELETON, permanent and survives trim: symptom, finding, workaround, solution, status, verdict, reviewed. IDENTITY: the entry tag, gloss, site, station where applicable, vintage as the frontier seal date, beartraps as links. FLESH: description, and description alone. TRIMMING AN ENTRY MEANS DELETING description AND NOTHING ELSE -- that is the whole operation, mechanical rather than a judgment about which sentences matter.":
            SchemaBirth="SKELETON ATTRIBUTES ARE WRITTEN TO SURVIVE ALONE. One sentence each, leaning on no prose, still legible in five months with nothing around it. symptom in particular is written as WHAT A FUTURE REMATCH WOULD GREP FOR, because a rematch is recognised by symptom and never by name -- nobody greps for bt34, they grep for the measurement destroyed its own subject. This is cheap to enforce at birth and impossible to retrofit, so the instrument is tombstone() in incant/lookup: it renders the entry with the prose already shed, which is exactly what the trim will leave. Render before you commit.";
            SchemaStatus="STATUS IS THE LIFECYCLE GRADE AND NOTHING ELSE, reusing the ratified NEXT grades so the project carries no third dialect: open, guess, ruled, remedy, plus baked and workaround. TWO TERMINAL FLAVOURS: remedy, and retired-unreproduced. They are different tombstones on purpose -- a problem retired because it was fixed and a problem retired because it could never be reproduced are not the same thing to a future reader, and the second is the one that comes back.";
            SchemaChannels="THREE FACTS, THREE CHANNELS, and this project has paid four times for putting two meanings on one. status is the lifecycle grade. verdict is Tony's disposition and is the LOUD channel -- it is where FIX THIS goes, and it stands verbatim once written. reviewed is the commentary ledger: append-only while the entry is live, collapsing to the final ruling at trim. History while it matters, tombstone after.";
            SchemaTrimGate="AN ENTRY IS TRIMMABLE WHEN status IS TERMINAL -- remedy or retired-unreproduced -- AND reviewed IS PAST ITS PLACEHOLDER, which is the exact token -- unreviewed --. The gate is Tony's and it is structural rather than remembered: a dot read of an absent attribute returns 0 cleanly, so description reading 0 IS the tombstone signal and a POP asserts the gate directly. The gate exists because four findings were withdrawn or inverted in one month and what caught every inversion was the surviving record of HOW the original conclusion was reached; trimming an active or freshly-settled entry destroys precisely that.";
            SchemaBirthRule="A NEW ID IS BORN BY ONE ACT WITH THREE EFFECTS: its one-sentence decoder line is written, AND its problem record is created with status open and explicit placeholders everywhere, AND the record is tombstone-rendered before it is committed. AN ID IN NEITHER PLACE IS NOT YET AN ID. Birth happens when a symptom is FIRST NAMED and not when the write-up happens, because an archive that only records finished fights is systematically missing every fight in progress -- which are the ones most likely to be re-encountered.";
            SchemaRematch="WHAT THE TRIMMED ARCHIVE BUYS, and it is the reason the tombstones are worth keeping at all: a HAVE WE FOUGHT THIS BEFORE surface. Before any campaign opens on a symptom, one grep across the tombstones answers whether this is a rematch, and the tombstone's solution attribute says how it was beaten last time. That is the known-positive-control doctrine getting a memory.";
            SchemaEmptyTrap="NO ATTRIBUTE IS EVER BORN AS AN EMPTY STRING. Measured 2026-08-23 on this file: a field with no data returns its OWN TAG, so reviewed set to an empty literal reads back as the string quoteBody and every non-empty check in the fleet passes on it. reviewed is born carrying the explicit placeholder -- unreviewed -- and the POP knows that token by name.";
        bt32 gloss="if/else breaks the parse" symptom="A define block fails silently and the run reports expected a method not X, where X is the FIRST action in the file and is perfectly healthy." finding="Observed once, 2026-08-21: a multi-statement indented if-arm followed by an else was reported to break the parse at define time, killing the whole define block. Unreproduced since across eight measured shapes." workaround="Set a flag to zero, set it in a single-statement if, then two separate ifs on its value; still standing in incant/frontier, frozen there by ruling rather than by need." solution="RETIRED-UNREPRODUCED, 2026-08-23. Six probe shapes and a full if/else conversion of the frontier itself all pass green, so no fix is owed and none is possible without a reproduction." status="retired-unreproduced" verdict="TONY, 2026-08-23: WTF. There is no way that any shape or form of an if/else statement should break the parse. That is unacceptable. Maybe there is a workaround. There should not have to be. FIX THIS. -- SUSPENDED, NOT WITHDRAWN, by the same-day regrade; the order stands verbatim and revives the moment a second sighting lands." reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="all nine, as the flag-plus-two-ifs shape they are written in" vintage="2026-08-22 SEQ 83" site="CLAUDE.md bear trap 32; probes at minionWork probe32a through probe32f and probe32copy" beartraps="bt4 bt29" description="THE EVIDENCE, and it is the reason this is retired rather than open. Six shapes were run against the current binary on 2026-08-23, all exit 0, all correct, all sentinels printed: a multi-statement indented arm with no else; a single-statement if with an else; a multi-statement arm THEN else, which is the shape said to fail; the same with a following action in the define block, which is the shape the reported symptom implies since it names the FIRST action; multi-statement arms on BOTH sides with the else arm taken; and a verbatim transplant of a frontier station rewritten to use if/else.
        THEN THE DECISIVE ONE. incant/frontier was copied to minionWork probe32copy and eight of its flag-plus-two-ifs pairs were mechanically converted to if/else, two of them producing the exact reported shape -- a multi-statement indented then-arm followed by an else, at stations 1 and 4. The copy ran all nine stations, all PASSED, ran-census 9 of 9, exit 0. The original was not touched and its md5 was checked before and after, because it remains the closest surviving relative of the destroyed 08-21 source.
        MISATTRIBUTION IS SUSPECTED AND IS NOT ESTABLISHED. The 2026-08-21 and 08-22 sessions withdrew or inverted four separate findings from a single confounder, and the frontier's own bt37 note records that replacing the multi-statement arm with two single-statement ifs produced NO CHANGE in the symptom it was being blamed for. The original state was destroyed and cannot be re-run, so this is a failure to reproduce and not a proof of absence.
        THIS TOMBSTONE IS THE KNOWN-POSITIVE CONTROL. A second sighting regrades this entry OPEN immediately and Tony's verdict revives with it.";
        bt34 gloss="truth-test fires action" symptom="A measurement destroys its own subject and then reports, correctly and uselessly, that the subject is empty." finding="Truth-testing a rule-shaped value is a CALL and not a read, because the if action evaluates its condition by calling the condition node's gMethod, and copyOf carries a rule's action along with the groupBody it copies wholesale." workaround="Never truth-test a rule-shaped value; assert instead on list length or on a CodE or BlocK reached by subscript, which a fired action cannot fake, and call for effect wherever the return value would itself be the rule." solution="Retires when the gMethod move lands and rule actions relocate to rStuff actionMethod, leaving the vacated gMethod at the structural default; that kills this and its statement-scope twin in one stroke." status="ruled" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="3, and it made station 4's failure a ghost" vintage="2026-08-22 SEQ 83" site="CLAUDE.md bear trap 34; GroupRules.mm:857 and :136" beartraps="bt37" description="WHAT IT COST IS THE ARGUMENT FOR THE WORKAROUND. A twin of Braced carries the Braced action, and that action is four lines: it clears its input and re-groups the node. So a station that asked did the copy work DESTROYED the copy, and the next station read the corpse and reported an install failure. The 2026-08-21 seal named that failure as the campaign's edge and asked what install requires that a copyOf twin lacks. The answer was NOTHING. A whole session's front question, generated by a one-line hazard three lines upstream.
        IT ALSO TOOK THE INVESTIGATION, which is why it is a trap and not a note. A probe wrote a truth-test on a returned rule, the test fired the action, the action wiped the CodE that had just been attached, and the probe then reported that no CodE was there. Knowing the hazard did not prevent it; only the shape did.";
        bt35 gloss="a rebind hands a copy" symptom="A write reports success and every read-back off the same local confirms it while the system never changed, or a chained subscript read returns a garbage token like xlInSet where a number was expected." finding="The rebind operator hands back a node that shares the original's child list but is not the original, and a chained subscript read in print or assignment position unwraps only once." workaround="Mutate the real node by direct subscript, read through a fresh capture taken immediately before each read, and ask every identity question in pointers through canonOf rather than by name." solution="RULED 2026-08-23: the rebind is MEANT to hand back the node, so copy-on-rebind is a defect and the repair is queued behind the seal with the JIT emitter as forcing function. The parenT half is separately open on question 3, whose measurement is named and standing." status="open" verdict="TONY, 2026-08-23 (R-f): symptom is not design. The rebind is meant to hand back the node; copy-on-rebind is the defect, not the documented behaviour. FENCED: this does not start the wrap/unwrap repair and does not fold it into the tokenize campaign -- the tokenize fix binds the real node despite the current rebind, per Ruling E, and does not require the rebind fixed. The record stays OPEN on question 3; stepped, not closed, remains the honest grade." reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="3 and 7" vintage="2026-08-22 SEQ 83" site="CLAUDE.md bear trap 35; runnable at incant/fixits/nodeIdentity" beartraps="bt26" description="THIS IS THE CONFOUNDER THAT ATE A WEEK, and no instrument caught it because a copy answers every READ correctly -- same tag, same flags, same list, same definingRule -- and diverges only on MUTATION, where the write lands on the copy and the read-back off that copy confirms it. No error, no crash, no diagnostic, fixture green.
        FOUR FINDINGS WERE REPORTED AND LATER INVERTED FROM THIS ONE ROOT: that the catalog face and the canon were different nodes; that setParse does not move the action; that commissioning breaks setParse; and that the fork reads null so binding is unreachable. The last sent a whole ruling to the architect's desk asking which node should carry a binding. The answer was the ordinary one, and nobody had ever actually written to it.
        THE FIXIT CARRIES FOUR ROADS and a killed candidate. Road 1, a direct subscript, reports SAME NODE. Road 2, the rebind, reports DIFFERENT NODES. Road 3, the parenT accessor, lands back on the child. Road 4, a chained read in assignment position, returns garbage. Road 3's mechanism was greped out on 2026-08-23 and looked clean -- opDot sets the product's group to the parent and unWrap walks while group-shaped, so it should overshoot into the parent's first member -- and road 3b KILLED IT by applying unWrap to Braced directly and getting Braced back. Whether road 3 is really road 2 wearing a mask is open; its first control went void because removing the rebind forced a chained read into the path.";
        bt37 gloss="artifact return unwinds caller" symptom="A block stops part-way through with no crash and no report, and the statements that vanish are the ones that would have said something was wrong." finding="A commissioned artifact's return unwinds the block that FIRED it, because a yield meaning leave the artifact is read as leave the enclosing block." workaround="Put verdicts and assertions one statement later, in a sibling block the artifact cannot unwind; the assertion is unchanged and only its seat moves." solution="Ruled: the machinery that fires a commissioned body consumes the body's return as a VALUE and never as control flow, so leaving the artifact terminates at the firing boundary. Landed and proven at parseRule's door only." status="ruled" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="6" vintage="2026-08-22 SEQ 83" site="CLAUDE.md bear trap 37" beartraps="bt34 oneChannelOneMeaning" description="⚠ DO NOT READ THE FRONTIER'S GREEN RUN AS THIS BEING CURED. Station 6's verdict prints today because the truth-test discipline removed the FIRE from that station, not because the unwind was contained. No artifact runs inside station 6 any more, so this path is NOT EXERCISED anywhere in that run, and a green run that never enters the failing path is not evidence the path is fixed.
        THE EVALUATION DOOR IS A LANGUAGE RULING AND NOT AN EDIT. Containment landed at the deliberate fire site. The other door is the if action's condition fork, and consuming a return there changes what if MEANS for every method-valued condition in the language. It is parked as a named acceptance criterion on the gMethod-move batch with a negative control attached: truth-test a commissioned BlocK, and it must NOT fire. Retirement fires on that verified landing and on nothing else.";
        F31 gloss="tokenize snake-eats-tail" symptom="Compiling the rule after an install fails at reached end of input on line 1, where the control arm fails on content." finding="CORRECTED, and the correction matters: it was never a body-misread. Installing a generated parse method gave the one BARE hook its FIRST attribute, which reclassified it from method-hook to attribute-matcher, so its method stopped being fired and the reader it served starved." workaround="Superseded. The staging apparatus -- off-rule storage, explicit activation, fourth customer, ruled-moment bind -- was proven on Braced and NEVER FIRED IN ANGER." solution="REMEDY: the dual citizenship was retired instead of the collision being armored against. tokenize now has zero reads-through and zero firings; the same-count discriminator that isolated F31 returns the SAME answer on both arms, which is F31's defining measurement returning null." status="remedy" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="the whole campaign is its proof" vintage="2026-08-23 closed" site="incant/grammar:34 tokenize, now fossil; GroupMain NamE and NumbeR, now dashed and term-free" beartraps="hookIdentityFork noActionNoHome boundarySupplyNotSubstitutable" description="THE CLOSING MEASUREMENTS.
        FOSSIL: fireLabelMethod tokenize firings ZERO over a real file; any tokenize activity ZERO; the rule still present at grammar:34, still bare (list 0, hasAttr 0), still tagged. Reads-through: NamE migrated, NumbeR migrated, FormaT closed, HeX parked.
        DISCRIMINATOR NULL: the same-count swap now answers identically on both arms -- both fail at else() AND followedBy() AND StatemenT(, the CONTENT signature. Before the campaign the tokenize arm answered reached-end-of-input.
        THE COUNT, taken on a per-target-isolated harness because compile-everything used to die at 12 of 43 and the number was unobtainable: 22 compiled clean, 19 parse-failed, 1 crashed, of 42 attempted.
        ⚠ THE 19 ARE WEATHER, NOT THE SNAKE, and they are named rather than absorbed: BasicElse CodE DEFINing DO DelimText ElseIf FOR IF InvokE Limit MemberS PrinT RunRulE Search SetBrackets TraiTdata WhilE break tokenize. The four that the 12-of-43 partial had already named -- BasicElse, CodE, DEFINing, DO -- are all present, which is what makes them pre-existing rather than caused. DatA crashes at 139 and is its own row. Each is an ordinary frontier row now, with no snake anywhere in it.
        ⚠ tokenize FAILING TO COMPILE IS EXPECTED AND HARMLESS: a bare rule generates a body with an empty condition. Nothing routes through it.
        THE FIX-SHAPE APPARATUS IS RECORDED SUPERSEDED-UNEXECUTED. It was correct, it was proven end-to-end on a specimen, and it was never needed -- the disease was cured by removing the dual role rather than by staging around it. It retires to doctrine.";
        twinBornDirty gloss="twin born carrying a StorE" symptom="A compile is handed two stored bodies and no coded body, and every reading taken off that state describes an artifact the harness itself made." finding="The specimen was minted AFTER a body had been hung on the live rule, so the copy was born carrying that body and the next station hung a second one onto it." workaround="None needed; the mint moved ahead of the hang, which removes the defect at source rather than cleaning up after it." solution="Landed 2026-08-22 as the first of three restructures." status="remedy" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="2 and 3, which swapped" vintage="2026-08-22 SEQ 83" site="incant/frontier stations 2 and 3" beartraps="none" description="AN ORDERING ARTIFACT OF THE HARNESS, NOT A SUBSTRATE DEFECT, and worth keeping as a tombstone for that reason: the readings it produced were about the instrument and were reported as being about the system. Swapping two stations removed it entirely.";
        codedFlagHandSet gloss="flag says coded, no artifact" symptom="A coded flag reads back set while there is no coded body for the compile to find." finding="The install hung the body by hand and set the coded flag separately, putting the claim and the artifact on two channels that could disagree." workaround="None; hand-setting is gone and the install goes through the shipped verb, whose last of three lines sets the flag that its first two lines earn." solution="Landed 2026-08-22 as the second of three restructures." status="remedy" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="4" vintage="2026-08-22 SEQ 83" site="incant/frontier station 4; GroupRules.mm:1893-1898" beartraps="oneChannelOneMeaning" description="THE TWO CHANNELS DISAGREED, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT: the flag said coded and there was no artifact to compile, so the station reported a failure that was an artifact of its own instrument. Making the flag and the artifact come from the same three lines means they cannot.";
        specimenNoRStuff gloss="specimen has no rStuff" symptom="A run exits 139 inside the rule-action runner and the failing station's own FAIL line never prints, so the harness's promise that the failing verdict is last on screen is broken." finding="Every generated body ends in a call that dereferences rStuff, and a copyOf twin has none, because copyOf copies the groupBody and the list while rStuff is neither." workaround="None was available; the crash was recorded rather than worked around, because the fix is C++ and gated on a build." solution="Ruling A and Ruling D1: a twin is a specimen and not an organism, presence of rStuff IS the liveness test, absence is lawful, and the runner returns trueResult on a specimen because ran successfully and nothing happened is the truthful report. Guard landed and the station is green." status="remedy" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="5" vintage="2026-08-22 SEQ 83" site="incant/frontier station 5; GroupRules.mm:12410" beartraps="none" description="THE BACKTRACE NAMED THE WRONG LINE, which is its own small lesson: the null arrives on one line and is dereferenced on the next, so the debugger points at the consumer and never at the producer. Harmless when reading a crash, not harmless when the line number becomes a control.";
        stubBreaksGrammar gloss="a stub breaks the grammar" symptom="Every construct written after a commissioning point fails to parse in the same file, including the one the next section needs to build its own trigger." finding="A bound parse method REPLACES the interpretive walk, so commissioning a stub onto a live grammar rule breaks the parser for everything written after it." workaround="The generated body is the real genParse shape rather than a stub, so the specimen does the rule's actual work." solution="Landed; and it is the staging hazard in its sharpest form, which is why the repair stages arming separately from routing." status="remedy" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="9" vintage="2026-08-22 SEQ 83" site="incant/frontier station 9" beartraps="F31" description="THE MECHANISM WAS NEVER AT FAULT. The same pipeline ran green with a genParse-shaped body in minionWork probeDecisiveV2, reading trigger parsed 1 and witness 987654 at exit 0. The frontier's body was simply the wrong body, and it broke the file's own later source.";
        bannerHole gloss="the banner lied once" symptom="A success banner claims every section passed while one of them never ran at all." finding="The dead flag only ever rises from a section that RAN, so a section whose body failed to parse was invisible to the completeness assertion." workaround="None; this one was fixed rather than lived with." solution="The foot prints a ran-census unconditionally and has three outcomes rather than two: stopped when a section said no, incomplete when a section never ran, and the success banner only when both are clear." status="remedy" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="the foot" vintage="2026-08-22 SEQ 83" site="incant/frontier frRun" beartraps="none" description="H2 EXACTLY, INSIDE AN INSTRUMENT BUILT TO PREVENT IT. The completeness assertion cannot see a section it never reached, so a headline count is a tally of the checks that RAN and says nothing about the checks that are missing from it.";
        hookIdentityFork gloss="install forks the hook's identity" symptom="A rule's catalog face and its canon report DIFFERENT NODES after an install that leaves every other installed rule reporting SAME NODE, and the rule's method stops being fired at all." finding="Install 43 gives the tokenize hook its FIRST attribute -- a CodE -- and across that install canonOf on the hook goes SAME NODE to DIFFERENT NODES, while the read-side walk that fired the hook's method one install earlier stops reaching it." workaround="None; this is a measurement, not a defect anyone is living with, and the campaign is gated on it." solution="NOT GRADED, deliberately. Arm A was chartered to confirm a read ENTERING the overwritten body; what it measured is the hook's method never being fired at all, which is a DIFFERENT mechanism from the one F31 predicts. Per the brief a divergence comes back to the table ungraded, so no mechanism is offered here." status="open" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="not a frontier station; measured on the real sweep" vintage="2026-08-23 Arm A" site="minionWork probeArmARead, probeArmA0, probeArmA1; read site GroupItem.twk:1000 fireLabelMethod" beartraps="F31 bt35" description="THE PROVENANCE TRIPLE.
        READ SITE: fireLabelMethod, GroupItem.twk:1000, generated into GroupItem.mm. It is the only site that fires a rule action, and it prints under traceParse.
        IDENTITY, IN POINTERS, WITH A CONTROL. Across the same sweep, in one process:
            Braced   installed at 3    face 0x102960300 canon 0x102960300  SAME NODE      before AND after
            NamE     in the sweep      face 0x1028c8400 canon 0x1028c8400  SAME NODE      before AND after
            tokenize installed at 43   face 0x1028c8780 canon 0x1028c8780  SAME NODE      BEFORE
            tokenize installed at 43   face 0x1028c8780 canon 0x102a4e240  DIFFERENT      AFTER
        So installation alone does NOT fork identity -- Braced was installed fortieth-from-last and stays SAME NODE. Only the hook forks. The new canon is NOT the attached CodE node either, which sits at 0x102a4e180, and it is stable across repeated resolutions rather than freshly minted per call.
        SEQUENCE POSITION: install 43 is the LAST of the sweep; the divergent read fires during the compile of BasicElse immediately after.
        THE SAME-COUNT SWAP AT THE READ SIDE, which is the discriminating evidence and is presence-with-value on the control arm rather than a bare absence claim. Both arms install FORTY TWO bodies; one member is exchanged:
            tokenize OUT   fireLabelMethod tokenize isMethod=1 label=1 -- fires, NamE completes, the read proceeds
            tokenize IN    tokenize is NEVER FIRED; the walk reaches first and nameSet, restarts, and dies at reached end of input line 1
        AND THE STRUCTURAL FACT UNDER IT: the hook's list length goes 0 to 1 across the install. A bare method hook carries no attributes; the install gives it one.
        ⚠ WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW, AND IT IS THE REASON THIS CAME BACK UNGRADED. F31 predicts a read ROUTING THROUGH the overwritten body -- entering a CodE that says how to parse the tokenize rule instead of how to tokenize. NOBODY HAS WATCHED THAT HAPPEN, and this measurement says the method is not entered at all. The failure is real, reproducible, isolated to the hook by a control, and located at a named read site. The mechanism is not the predicted one and no replacement mechanism is offered, because the last several times a mechanism was reasoned out on this project rather than run, it was wrong.";
        canonRoadDependence gloss="the road mints the face" symptom="Two identity censuses of the same nodes in the same binary disagree, and the rules that changed are the ones reached by a different spelling rather than the ones treated." finding="canonOf reports face-versus-canon, and the FACE depends on the road the node travelled to reach it: an action-argument road mints a fresh face so a healthy rule reads DIFFERENT NODES, while a direct subscript on the same rule in the same process reads SAME NODE." workaround="An identity census fixes ONE road for every row, before and after, and says in the report which road it fixed; rows taken by different roads are not comparable even within one run." solution="NOT GRADED. It is adjacent to the ruled copy-on-rebind defect and may be the same disease seen from another side, but the fence stands and nothing was chased." status="open" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="not a frontier station; surfaced by M3's instrument check" vintage="2026-08-23 M3" site="minionWork probeM3; canonOf at GroupRules.mm" beartraps="bt35 hookIdentityFork" description="MEASURED IN ONE PROCESS, BOTH ROADS, BEFORE AND AFTER THE SAME SWEEP.
        direct subscript   Braced   face 0x104f9c300 canon 0x104f9c300  SAME       before AND after
        direct subscript   tokenize face 0x1046ac780 canon 0x1046ac780  SAME       before
        direct subscript   tokenize face 0x1046ac780 canon 0x10509c3c0  DIFFERENT  after
        action argument    Braced   face 0x105047400 canon 0x104f9c300  DIFFERENT  before AND after
        So on the argument road Braced is indistinguishable from the post-install hook, and a census taken that way has NO discriminating power while looking exactly like one that does.
        ⚠ IT CAUGHT A LIVE REPORT. The Arm A census used the direct-subscript road for every row and its finding -- installation alone does not fork identity, only the hook forks -- REPRODUCES on that road and STANDS. A follow-up probe used the argument road, read DIFFERENT for the controls too, and appeared to contradict it. The contradiction was the instrument. Doubt-the-instrument in its cheap direction: one probe printing both roads settled in a single run what would otherwise have been a withdrawn finding.";
        percentMinusClosesPassthrough gloss="a width flag ends the block" symptom="tok prints Expected a semi-colon and the extern count in the generated header drops to zero, after an edit that added only well-formed C++ inside a passthrough." finding="A percent followed by a minus is the tok passthrough CLOSE delimiter, so a printf left-justify width flag inside a passthrough ends the block early and the remaining C++ is parsed as tok source." workaround="Never use a left-justify flag in a format string inside a passthrough; pad by other means. The same sequence cannot appear in a COMMENT inside the passthrough either, because comments are parsed and not skipped." solution="None owed; it follows from the delimiter choice and no ruling has been asked for. Detector is the standing canary -- count the externs in the generated header after every retok." status="workaround" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="not a frontier station; surfaced building the SEQ 85 probe" vintage="2026-08-23 SEQ 85" site="ruleActions.rtn aCTionNamE; detector grep -c on GroupRules.h" beartraps="bt29 bt4" description="⚠ THE RECORD OF THIS ENTRY IS THE METHOD, NOT THE TRAP. Three retoks failed at 315 to 0 and TWO CAUSES WERE DIAGNOSED AND BOTH WERE WRONG -- first that a declared type-name inside the passthrough comment was being parsed, second that the block sat inside the declaration block. The third change fixed it, which under the usual reading would have canonised whichever theory was live.
        THREE CONTROLS SETTLED IT INSTEAD, one retok each:
            A  comment WITH declared type-names, below the declarations, no width flag   315  fine
            B  no comment, ABOVE the declaration it was blamed for, no width flag        315  fine
            C  the known-good file with ONLY the width flag restored                       0  fatal
        So both diagnoses were false and the width flag is the sole cause. The cost of the controls was three retoks; the cost of not running them would have been two wrong mechanisms written into the register as fact.
        ⚠ AND IT DEMONSTRATED ITSELF ONCE MORE, IMMEDIATELY. The comment written to warn the next reader SPELLED THE SEQUENCE, and the very next retok went to zero again. Comments are parsed, not skipped, so the warning was the wound. The comment now describes the two characters in words and cannot contain them.";
        noActionNoHome gloss="no action, no home" symptom="A per-rule migration verifies on the rules that have actions and has nowhere to land on the rules that do not." finding="Of the four rules carrying a tokenize term, only NamE and NumbeR have actions -- HeX and FormaT read isMethod 0 -- so a self-stamp shaped as the-action-gains-a-block covers half the population and the end state that needs all four is unreachable in that shape." workaround="None attempted. NamE was migrated and verified; the remaining three were not started, because inventing two actions purely to carry a stamp is a design decision and not an execution one." solution="DISCHARGED 2026-08-23 by the boundary trigger. captureSpan is invoked from fireLabelMethod ABOVE the isMethod-and-label guard, so it never consults the action population at all -- an action-less rule gets its span by exactly the same path as any other. The precondition that could not be met was dissolved rather than satisfied." status="remedy" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="not a frontier station; surfaced opening SEQ 86" vintage="2026-08-23 SEQ 86" site="ruleActions.rtn has aCTionNamE and aCTionNumbeR only; incant/grammar:90 and :101 define HeX and FormaT" beartraps="F31 hookIdentityFork" description="MEASURED AT RUNTIME WITH CONTROLS, not inferred from a grep.
            NamE      isMethod 1   list 3   hasAttr 1
            NumbeR    isMethod 1   list 3   hasAttr 1
            HeX       isMethod 0   list 4   hasAttr 1
            FormaT    isMethod 0   list 6   hasAttr 1
            Braced    isMethod 1   -- control, a rule known to carry an action
            tokenize  isMethod 1   -- control, the hook itself is a method
        ⚠ AND IT EXPLAINS WHY THE HOOK EXISTS AT ALL. A shared TERM can be added to any rule without that rule owning an action; that is precisely the reuse tokenize provides, and it is the same property that gives it the dual role. So the mechanism that makes the hazard is the mechanism that makes the coverage, and removing one costs the other unless the capture moves somewhere every rule reaches.";
        boundaryStampIsTermLevel gloss="term-level, not rule-level" symptom="A migration planned as extending shipped boundary machinery finds the machinery stamps a different population than the one it needs." finding="Every shipped setToken writer in the parse machinery is a CHARACTER-SET TERM primitive on one arm or the other; testOptions and testAttributes -- the two arms that match RULES -- write no span at all, so no shipped mechanism stamps a rule's product with a parent-level span except tokenize itself." workaround="None; nothing was built. Phase 1's gate tripped on its first condition and the brief stopped there by its own terms." solution="NOT GRADED. Home 2 remains the ruled destination but is a NEW mechanism at the rule boundary rather than a widening of an existing one, and its mark source at that site is unmeasured." status="open" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="not a frontier station; SEQ 87 phase 1" vintage="2026-08-23 SEQ 87" site="RuleStuff.twk:314 in the testMacro; Generate.rtn:94/118/268; candidate boundary GroupItem.twk:1336 and :1387" beartraps="noActionNoHome F31" description="⚠ THIS ENTRY CORRECTS ONE OF MY OWN, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE ENTRY'S REASON FOR EXISTING. The SEQ 84 M4 report said home 2 ALREADY EXISTS AND SHIPS, ON BOTH ARMS, and named these same sites. That was materially overstated and a whole brief's optimistic branch was built on it -- the phase-1 clause reading if the delta is genuinely one parent-level entry mark, say so came straight from my line.
        THE COMPLETE CENSUS, both languages, whole tree, with the control sites named before it ran and all present:
            RuleStuff.twk:314     the testMacro, expanded at :325 :334 :344    character terms, interpretive
            Generate.rtn:94/118/268   parseAny parseCharacter parseSet         character terms, generated
            GroupActions.rtn:1572     tokenize                                 the ONLY shipped rule-product span
            ruleActions.rtn:284       aCTionCodE                               one rule's bespoke bracket scan
            Instruct.rtn:44           getMarkLineAt                            a utility, not parse machinery
        testOptions and testAttributes contain ZERO setToken calls. Those are the arms that match rules.
        AND THE RUNTIME PROOF WAS ALREADY IN HAND, stronger than any grep: de-terming NamE left its product with NO token, so the first statement of its action read null and the process exited 139. Had any shipped machinery stamped that product, it could not have crashed. An absence that crashes is presence-with-value at its loudest.
        THE SPAN SHAPES ALSO DIFFER, which is the second half of the delta: the shipped term stamp writes the TERM's own hereAt plus a match counter, on success only and only if the term carries a label. The proven rule formula is the PARENT's hereAt to the rule's own atRuleMark. Those are not the same span and one does not widen into the other.";
        parentPopulationSplit gloss="two splits, misaligned" symptom="A change made in the grammar file has no effect on some rules and full effect on others, and a sweep census disagrees with a definition census about which rules exist." finding="The four rules carrying a tokenize term split two different ways that do not line up: NamE and NumbeR are bootstrap-built in C++ and their grammar-file lines are INERT, while NamE and FormaT are never installed by the sweep -- so a definition-time hook reaches one half and the generator reaches a different half." workaround="None; nothing was built on either assumption. It is named so the 54-of-54 endgame does not trip over it." solution="NOT GRADED and the diagnosis is unowned. The installation half is suspected to be one of fbWalk's guards (datA, noPrinT, binTypE, isRulE, actionTypE) and that is a suspicion, not a measurement." status="open" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="not a frontier station; SEQ 88 B4 and SEQ 89 C1" vintage="2026-08-23 SEQ 89" site="GroupMain.twk:212 and :225 build NamE and NumbeR; incant/grammar:35 and :38 are the inert lines; incant/grammar:90 and :101 define HeX and FormaT" beartraps="noActionNoHome boundaryStampIsTermLevel" description="MEASURED WITH A POSITIVE CONTROL, which is the only reason the inert half is believable. One optional term was added to the grammar file's NamE, NumbeR and HeX lines and the rules were re-read:
            HeX      list 4 -> 5    the grammar line IS live, aCTionDefinE runs
            NamE     list 2 -> 2    INERT
            NumbeR   list 3 -> 3    INERT
            FormaT   list 6 -> 6    untouched, the control that stayed put
        HeX moving is what makes NamE and NumbeR standing still a finding rather than a broken probe.
        THE TWO SPLITS, side by side, and they do not agree:
            rule      defined by                installed by the sweep
            NamE      GroupMain, C++            no
            NumbeR    GroupMain, C++            yes
            HeX       grammar, aCTionDefinE     yes
            FormaT    grammar, aCTionDefinE     no
        ⚠ SO A RULED PREMISE IS FALSE AS STATED: grammar is not the sole source of truth for this population. Any constitutional fact declared in the grammar reaches HeX and FormaT only, and a second site in the C++ bootstrap is required for NamE and NumbeR -- one fact, two homes, which is the shape this project has a standing rule against.";
        unregisteredIsATerm gloss="unregistered becomes a term" symptom="A rule silently grows extra terms, a generated method indexes past its real term count, and the run dies at SIGSEGV somewhere unrelated." finding="A declaration written into the grammar that is not registered as a command in incant/setup is never fired and never consumed -- the parser meets it as an ORDINARY TERM and adds it to the rule's term list." workaround="Register the word as a command WHERE THE GRAMMAR IS READ, not only inside the fixture that tests it. The detector is the rule's term-list length: a consumed declaration leaves it unmoved, an unconsumed one grows it." solution="None owed; it follows from commands being resolved by registration. It is doctrine to apply, not a defect to fix." status="workaround" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="not a frontier station; re-read for SEQ 90 D1" vintage="2026-08-23 SEQ 90" site="incant/setup, the install-vocabulary block around line 56" beartraps="parentPopulationSplit" description="⚠ IT IS THE LOAD-BEARING FACT UNDER THE WHOLE TOKENize FLOOR DESIGN, which is why it is minted rather than left in a source comment. That design's entire claim is that the declaration has NO match-time existence -- nothing in any term list, nothing the walk meets, nothing the generator must elide. Every one of those properties is bought by the registration and by nothing else. An unregistered TOKENize would be a term in four grammar rules at once.
        WHAT IT COST WHEN IT WAS LEARNED, per setup's own comment: a parseMethod declaration written into incant/grammar was registered only inside the fixture that tested it, so where the grammar is actually read it was not a command. Braced went from THREE terms to FIVE against a generated method indexing rule[1..3], and oneTest died at SIGSEGV.
        MEASURED AGAIN 2026-08-23 AS A CONTROL PAIR, on FormaT's live grammar line:
            an ordinary optional term added    list 6 -> 7    grows
