        parseSelfRecursion gloss="generated parse re-enters itself" symptom="A generated-parse walk completes and prints its sentinel, and then the verification call dies at SIGSEGV with tens of thousands of identical stack frames naming one action." finding="CONFIRMED BY PRINT 2026-08-27, in pointers. aCTionStatemenT dispatches statement->groupBody->gMethod(statement) at GroupRules.mm:1487; under a walked-and-compiled grammar that gMethod resolves to aCTionStatemenT itself, and the node handed down is THE SAME NODE every time -- input == statement true, and input identical at 0x...5f140 across 35 consecutive frames read at depths 6, 8, 20 and 30, tag StatemenT throughout. Period one, in pointers rather than by frame-shape inference. ⚠ AND getGroup IS NOT INVOLVED AT ALL: StatemenT reads datA 0, not isGROUP, so the guard above it is false and the call never fires. statement is the plain assignment from input and nothing between touches it. 74705 frames, guard page touched by the allocation at GroupRules.mm:1475." workaround="Turn either gate off. The crash needs BOTH compiling and activating on; any other cell of the 2x2 completes at exit 0 and reaches the verify sentinel." solution="CANDIDATE 2 PROMOTED TO RULED, 2026-08-27, on the confirming print. The mechanism is settled: one node, its own action, dispatched at GroupRules.mm:1487. ⚠ THE FIX SHAPE IS NOT SELECTED -- the ruling is about the DIAGNOSIS, and the fix conversation opens on that dispatch line. Nothing is buildable from this row yet." status="ruled" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="not a frontier station; parser iteration 2, 2026-08-26; confirming print 2026-08-27" vintage="2026-08-27" site="GroupRules.mm:1487 is the recursing dispatch; GroupRules.mm:1475 is the allocation that touches the guard page; IncantForms/WorkingOn/parser is the caller, with BOTH gates on. ⚠ ERRATUM: the 2026-08-26 docket cited :1483 for getGroup. At that same vintage getGroup is at :1484 and :1483 is the isGROUP guard above it -- bear-trap 36 exactly, a line number naming the test rather than the call. The call is moot in any case; it never fires." beartraps="bt34 bt26" description="⚠ THE PRINT, 2026-08-27, AND IT ANSWERED BOTH HALVES AND CORRECTED THE QUESTION. Chartered as: what does getGroup hand back, and is the statement arriving at gMethod the same node that entered the frame above. ANSWER ONE, same node, in pointers -- input == statement reads TRUE and the pointer is byte-identical across 35 consecutive frames sampled at four depths. ANSWER TWO, getGroup hands back NOTHING, because it is never called: StatemenT reads datA 0 and the isGROUP guard above the call is false. So there is no unwrapping step to blame and no cycle to trace -- the loop is as short as a loop can be. A node whose gMethod is aCTionStatemenT, handed to aCTionStatemenT, which dispatches its input gMethod on its input.
        ⚠ THE SPECIMEN HAD A NEW BODY AND THE BEHAVIOUR DID NOT MOVE. Between the docket and the print, connectiveDiscriminants remedy changed StatemenTs emitted parse from a five-term AND to a five-term OR. Re-run in the crashing configuration it dies identically -- walk completes, PARSER SENTINEL and WITNESS print, then 139 at the verify call, 74705 frames against the dockets 74702. The three-frame difference is entry-path depth at the moment the guard page is touched. Recorded because it was asked for, and it is a NEGATIVE result: the connective is not a variable here.
        THE DOCKET. WHO: StatemenT. traceParse names it as the last rule entered before death -- runRule DOOR on StatemenT field= 1 fieldData= 1 -- and the repeating frame is its own action. ⚠ WHERE EXECUTION WAS IS NOT YET WHO POOCHED IT, and the obvious control does NOT isolate it: parser(StatemenT) walks the same 123 rules as parser(Start) and crashes identically, because Start reaches everything through StatemenT. So StatemenT is confirmed as the site and NOT as the discriminator.
        WHERE: frames 4 and up are aCTionStatemenT at GroupRules.mm:1487:11, repeating to frame 74702. Frame 4 is aCTionStatemenT at :1475:26 in operator new, the allocation that finally touched the guard page. The stack bottom is ordinary: main to parse to fireLabelMethod to aCTionRunRulE to runRule, which is the parser driver line, then aCTionXpress to runOP to runAction into the action body.
        WHAT: signal 11, bad pointer dereference at a stack address, two runs differing only by stack ASLR. It is a STACK OVERFLOW FROM UNBOUNDED RECURSION and not a null read, and the frame count is what says so.
        WHEN: after PARSER SENTINEL and after WITNESS compiled 1, and before VERIFY SENTINEL. So the walk is complete and the crash is inside the verification call, not inside generation.
        ⚠ THE 2x2 IS THE SHARPEST FACT AND IT IS A REAL DISCRIMINATOR. Both gates are required:
            compiling 1 activating 1   CRASHES, 123 rules walked
            compiling 0 activating 1   completes, 123 walked, verify sentinel reached
            compiling 1 activating 0   completes, 115 walked, verify sentinel reached
            compiling 0 activating 0   completes, 115 walked, answer xl1
        A body that is bound but not compiled does not crash, and a body that is compiled but not bound does not crash. Only a body that is both armed to dispatch AND compiled to a runnable block does.
        CANDIDATE READINGS, MARKED AS CANDIDATES. Strongest is BEST GUESS and nothing here is ruled.
        BEST GUESS -- gMethod carried by copyOf, the bt34 family. The parser installs its generated body as codeCopy from copyOf(CodE), and copyOf copies groupBody wholesale, gMethod included. If the installed node reaches line 1487 carrying aCTionStatemenT as its own gMethod, the dispatch is a self-call by construction. What would promote it: read gMethod on the installed CodE copy against the shared grammar CodE. What would kill it: finding the installed copy carries a different gMethod, or none.
        CANDIDATE -- the getGroup at :1483 returns the node it was handed. Line 1483 replaces statement with statement->getGroup() when the data is a group, and the dispatch two lines later is on the result. A generated body whose match leaves the group pointing at the rule itself would make the replacement a no-op and the dispatch a self-call, with no copied method involved. What would separate this from the above: whether the recursion is on ONE node or walks a chain.
        NOTED, NOT GRADED -- StatemenT is generated with AND rather than OR. Its emitted body is if SemI() AND BlocK() AND WardeD() AND Iterate() AND Xpress(), because generateParse picks the conjunct on hasAttributeS and StatemenT carries attributes. If members are rule options then an alternation emitted as a conjunction is wrong on its own terms. It is recorded here because it was seen while docketing and NOT because it is believed to cause the recursion; it would produce a wrong parse, not an unbounded one.
        ⚠ WHAT WAS NOT DONE, deliberately, per the terminal condition: no fix, no verdict, no emitter edit. The trace arm answered on the first run, so the second and third resorts named in the brief -- stamping an entry print into every generated tail, and Xcode -- were not needed and were not used.

        ========================================================================
        THE PROBE STATION, 2026-08-26. Four probes, chartered as classification
        and not pursuit. CLASSIFICATION: LOCAL.

        PROBE 1, CYCLE STRUCTURE -- PERIOD ONE. Frames 5 through 46 are 42
        CONSECUTIVE aCTionStatemenT frames, identical signature, identical +364
        offset, with NOTHING between them. A cycle whose period exceeded one
        could not produce 42 consecutive identical frames, so this is decisive
        for the visible window regardless of the elision below it. The entire
        printed backtrace holds only 18 distinct frame signatures, and
        fireLabelMethod appears exactly TWICE -- both in the stack BOTTOM, at
        frames 74692 and 74699, on the ordinary path into the driver line. It
        is not inside the repeating unit. Candidate 3 predicted a cycle whose
        period includes label machinery; that signature is ABSENT.

        PROBE 2, gMethod IDENTITY -- CANDIDATE 1 IS FALSIFIED, and it was the
        docket's BEST GUESS. Read as pointers, before and after generation:
            shared grammar CodE   gMethod 0x100380ec0
            installed CodE copy   gMethod 0x0        rStuff 0x0
            StatemenT before      gMethod 0x100386458
            StatemenT after       gMethod 0x100386458   node and body unmoved
        copyOf did NOT carry gMethod onto the installed copy. The bt34-family
        reading is dead, and generation does not alter StatemenT's own gMethod
        or its identity. Candidate 2 -- the dispatch target reached through the
        getGroup at line 1483 already carrying aCTionStatemenT -- now stands
        alone among the three.

        PROBE 3, parentLabel CENSUS -- THE POPULATION IS EMPTY. StatemenT holds
        five members, SemI BlocK WardeD Iterate Xpress, and every one reads
        parentLabel 0x0, as does StatemenT itself. Candidate 3 requires that
        population to be nonempty. Read at rest, before any generation, which
        is what the charter asked for; it does not exclude a parentLabel set
        during a parse.

        PROBE 4, FORWARD REFERENCE -- MIXED, AND THE MIXTURE IS THE FINDING.
        DEFINing compiled STANDALONE walks exactly ONE rule, so no neighbour
        has a generated body, and it refuses exactly as it does inside the full
        walk. So a refusal does NOT need neighbours to exist, and the current
        interleaved walk already satisfies eager compilation -- no generate-all
        pass is required for forward references. BUT Xpress refuses inside the
        123-rule walk and compiles CLEAN standalone at 35 rules. So the walk is
        not context-free either, and the direction is the opposite of a
        forward-reference shortage: MORE armed and compiled neighbours produce
        MORE refusals, not fewer. That is the same seam the 2x2 names.

        THE 42 REFUSALS DECOMPOSE, current binary: 36 reached end of input, 4
        empty condition, 2 real mid-condition stop.

        ⚠ WHY LOCAL AND NOT STRUCTURAL, stated plainly because the exit table
        turns on it: the structural exit was reserved for a cycle running
        through the label machinery, and probes 1 and 3 both say it is not
        there -- period one, and an empty parentLabel population. What remains
        is a single frame dispatching into itself, which is the local reading.
        ⚠ AND THE HONEST QUALIFIER: probe 4 found ONE context-dependent rule,
        which is a structural smell sitting next to a local crash. It is
        reported rather than resolved, because chasing it is pursuit and the
        charter forbade it.";
        bt4 gloss="comment kills an if" symptom="A parse fails near a line that looks innocent, and field-resolution bleed cascades into the externs that follow it." finding="A line comment wedged between an if's condition and its governed statement is consumed AS that statement, orphaning the real one, because tok and incant have no lexer and comments are parsed rather than skipped." workaround="Put a comment only where a statement is legal, and near an if put it above the whole statement rather than inside it." solution="None owed; the behaviour follows from the single-pass no-lexer design and no ruling has been asked for." status="workaround" verdict="-- awaiting Tony --" reviewed="-- unreviewed --" station="not met on the frontier" vintage="predates the frontier" site="CLAUDE.md bear trap 4" beartraps="bt29 bt32" description="THE HEURISTIC THAT MAKES IT MEMORABLE IS TONY'S: put a comment only where a STATEMENT is allowed. tok parses a comment into a statement slot, so it is safe anywhere a statement is legal, and the failure is precisely where the parser expects the governed statement.";
